## Basic usage

```tsx
import Link from "next/link";

export function Nav() {
  return (
    <nav>
      <Link href="/">Home</Link>
      <Link href="/about">About</Link>
      <Link href="/products/42">Product 42</Link>
    </nav>
  );
}
```

In the browser this renders as `<a href="/about">About</a>` — a normal anchor, indexable and keyboard-accessible. Clicking it is intercepted by Next.js's client router instead of causing a full page reload.

## Why not a plain anchor?

| Behaviour | `<a href>` | `<Link href>` |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Full page reload | Yes | No |
| Layout remount | Yes | No (persistent layouts stay mounted) |
| Prefetching | No | Yes (in viewport) |
| Client state preserved | No | Yes |
| Works without JS | Yes | Yes (degrades to anchor) |

The plain anchor reloads the whole page, remounts every component, and loses all client state. Link does a partial update — only the changed route segment re-renders.

## Prefetching

Links visible in the viewport are prefetched in the background by default in production:

```tsx
// Opt out for a link that's expensive to prefetch
<Link href="/heavy-page" prefetch={false}>
  Heavy page
</Link>
```

Prefetching downloads the static parts of the destination route. When the user clicks, the page loads near-instantly because the data is already in the browser's cache.

## Dynamic routes

```tsx
<Link href={`/products/${product.id}`}>{product.name}</Link>

// Or with the href object form (useful for complex URLs)
<Link
  href={{
    pathname: "/products/[id]",
    query: { id: product.id },
  }}
>
  {product.name}
</Link>
```

## Active link styling

Use `usePathname` (client-side hook) to detect the active route:

```tsx
"use client";
import Link from "next/link";
import { usePathname } from "next/navigation";

export function NavLink({
  href,
  children,
}: {
  href: string;
  children: React.ReactNode;
}) {
  const pathname = usePathname();
  const isActive = pathname === href;

  return (
    <Link
      href={href}
      className={isActive ? "font-bold text-blue-600" : "text-gray-600"}
    >
      {children}
    </Link>
  );
}
```

## replace vs push

By default Link pushes a new history entry. Pass `replace` to replace instead (useful for redirects after form submit):

```tsx
<Link href="/dashboard" replace>
  Go to dashboard
</Link>
```

## Programmatic navigation

For navigation triggered by code (after a form submit, for example), use `useRouter`:

```tsx
"use client";
import { useRouter } from "next/navigation";

export function SubmitButton() {
  const router = useRouter();

  async function handleSubmit() {
    await saveForm();
    router.push("/success");
  }

  return <button onClick={handleSubmit}>Submit</button>;
}
```

## The public/ folder

Anything in `public/` is served verbatim from the root of the site:

```
public/
├── favicon.ico           → /favicon.ico
├── robots.txt            → /robots.txt
├── og-image.png          → /og-image.png
├── fonts/
│   └── custom.woff2      → /fonts/custom.woff2
└── icons/
    └── apple-touch-icon.png → /icons/apple-touch-icon.png
```

No import, no build step. The browser just requests the URL.

## Referencing from code

```tsx
// In JSX — relative to the public root
<img src="/logo.svg" alt="Logo" />

// In metadata
export const metadata = {
  openGraph: {
    images: [{ url: "/og-image.png", width: 1200, height: 630 }],
  },
};
```

## What belongs here vs next/image

| Use case | Approach |
| --- | --- |
| Favicon, apple-touch-icon | `public/` + metadata config |
| robots.txt, sitemap.xml | `public/` (or generate via route handler) |
| Open Graph images | `public/` or generated via `app/opengraph-image.tsx` |
| User-facing images (hero, product, avatar) | `next/image` for optimisation |
| Fonts | `next/font` (preferred) or `public/fonts/` + `@font-face` |

The key rule: `public/` is for files that should be served **verbatim** at a **fixed URL**. Anything that benefits from being resized, re-encoded, or lazy-loaded should go through `next/image`.

## Generating static files programmatically

App Router lets you generate `robots.txt` and `sitemap.xml` via special file conventions:

```ts
// app/robots.ts
import type { MetadataRoute } from "next";

export default function robots(): MetadataRoute.Robots {
  return {
    rules: { userAgent: "*", allow: "/" },
    sitemap: "https://example.com/sitemap.xml",
  };
}
```

```ts
// app/sitemap.ts
import type { MetadataRoute } from "next";

export default async function sitemap(): Promise<MetadataRoute.Sitemap> {
  const posts = await getAllPosts();
  return posts.map((post) => ({
    url: `https://example.com/blog/${post.slug}`,
    lastModified: post.updatedAt,
  }));
}
```

These generate the correct response at build time (for static) or per-request (for dynamic). Preferred over static files in `public/` for content that needs to reflect the current database state.

## What you cannot do

- Put files outside `public/` and expect them to be served — only the `public/` folder is exposed
- Rename `public/` — it's a fixed convention
- Use `public/` files inside CSS `url()` in CSS Modules (use an absolute path string like `url('/bg.png')`)

## Where it lives and what it is

`next.config.js` (or `.ts` in newer setups) sits at the project root alongside `package.json`. It exports a plain JavaScript/TypeScript object (or a function) that Next.js merges with its defaults:

```ts
// next.config.ts
import type { NextConfig } from "next";

const config: NextConfig = {
  // your overrides here
};

export default config;
```

## Common configuration areas

### Allowed image origins

```ts
const config: NextConfig = {
  images: {
    remotePatterns: [
      {
        protocol: "https",
        hostname: "images.example.com",
        pathname: "/uploads/**",
      },
      {
        hostname: "cdn.shopify.com",
      },
    ],
  },
};
```

Requried for any external `src` passed to `<Image>`. Prevents your origin's Image Optimisation API from being used as an open proxy.

### Redirects and rewrites

```ts
const config: NextConfig = {
  async redirects() {
    return [
      {
        source: "/old-page",
        destination: "/new-page",
        permanent: true, // 308
      },
    ];
  },

  async rewrites() {
    return [
      {
        source: "/api/proxy/:path*",
        destination: "https://external-api.com/:path*",
      },
    ];
  },
};
```

Redirects change the URL in the browser. Rewrites proxy silently — the URL stays the same.

### Custom HTTP headers

```ts
const config: NextConfig = {
  async headers() {
    return [
      {
        source: "/(.*)",
        headers: [
          { key: "X-Frame-Options", value: "DENY" },
          { key: "X-Content-Type-Options", value: "nosniff" },
          {
            key: "Content-Security-Policy",
            value: "default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' 'unsafe-eval'",
          },
        ],
      },
    ];
  },
};
```

Security headers that would otherwise need a reverse proxy.

### Standalone output (for Docker)

```ts
const config: NextConfig = {
  output: "standalone",
};
```

Packages only the runtime you need into `.next/standalone/`. Copy it into a Docker image and run it without installing `node_modules`.

### Base path and asset prefix

```ts
const config: NextConfig = {
  basePath: "/app",       // serve at /app instead of /
  assetPrefix: "https://cdn.example.com", // serve static assets from CDN
};
```

### Turbopack (dev, Next 15+)

```ts
const config: NextConfig = {
  // Turbopack is on by default in Next.js 15 dev
  // Configure it here
  turbopack: {
    rules: {
      "*.svg": {
        loaders: ["@svgr/webpack"],
        as: "*.js",
      },
    },
  },
};
```

### Webpack extension

```ts
const config: NextConfig = {
  webpack(config, { isServer }) {
    // Add a rule, alias, plugin…
    config.module.rules.push({
      test: /\.svg$/,
      use: ["@svgr/webpack"],
    });
    return config; // must return the modified config
  },
};
```

Use this only when Turbopack can't handle what you need — Webpack extensions slow down builds.

## Reading environment variables

```ts
// next.config.ts
const config: NextConfig = {
  env: {
    // Exposed to the browser (same as NEXT_PUBLIC_)
    APP_VERSION: process.env.APP_VERSION ?? "local",
  },
};
```

Secrets should stay in `.env.local` and be read in Server Components or Route Handlers — not baked into the config.
